---
import DeepDiveLayout from '../../layouts/DeepDiveLayout.astro';
---

<DeepDiveLayout
  title="Deep Dive into the Load"
  pillar="Load"
  chapterNumber="01"
  nextTitle="Balance"
  nextHref="/learn/balance"
>
  <section>
    <h2>Quick Recap</h2>

    <p>
      The load prepares the body for explosive movement.
    </p>

    <p>
      A good load builds energy while keeping the hitter athletic, balanced,
      and prepared to support every pillar that follows.
    </p>

    <p>
      For the purposes of Launch Baseball, the load includes every movement
      that prepares the body to fire and ends at heel plant.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Before We Begin</h2>

    <p>
      There are countless ways to load.
    </p>

    <p>
      Some hitters use no stride. Others use a small stride, a toe tap, or a
      larger leg lift. At first glance, these movements may look completely
      different.
    </p>

    <p>
      They are not trying to accomplish different goals.
    </p>

    <p>
      They are simply using different methods to solve the same problem.
    </p>

    <p>
      At Launch Baseball, we do not judge the appearance of a hitter's load.
      We judge what it accomplishes.
    </p>

    <p>
      A quality load prepares the body for explosive movement while supporting
      every pillar that follows.
    </p>

    <p>
      If it builds energy, maintains balance, supports an efficient firing
      sequence, and places the hands in a position that supports a quality
      barrel path, then it is accomplishing its purpose.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      The method is less important than the result.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common Loading Techniques</h2>

    <p>
      There is no single correct loading style.
    </p>

    <p>
      Different hitters can solve the same problem in different ways.
    </p>

    <h3>No Stride</h3>

    <p>
      A no-stride load minimizes forward movement while still allowing the
      hitter to gather, build energy, and prepare to fire.
    </p>

    <h3>Small Stride</h3>

    <p>
      A small stride creates rhythm and controlled movement without requiring
      a large change in position.
    </p>

    <h3>Toe Tap</h3>

    <p>
      A toe tap allows the hitter to begin the load by gathering before moving forward.
    </p>

    <h3>Larger Leg Lift</h3>

    <p>
      Some successful hitters naturally use a larger leg lift.
    </p>

    <p>
      Like every other loading style, it should be evaluated by the same
      principles.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      The size of the leg lift is not the objective. The quality of the load is.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What We Want From the Load</h2>

    <p>
      Every quality load should accomplish the same basic objectives.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Build energy.</li>
      <li>Create movement before explosive movement.</li>
      <li>Maintain an athletic and balanced position.</li>
      <li>Keep the head neutral and both eyes available to track the baseball.</li>
      <li>Place the hands in a position that supports an efficient barrel path.</li>
      <li>Leave the body prepared to fire efficiently from the ground up.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="callout">
      The load should strengthen every pillar that follows—not make them more
      difficult.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Timing the Load</h2>

    <p>
      The load should be completed in time to handle the fastest pitch that
      pitcher can throw.
    </p>

    <p>
      The hitter needs to know the pitcher's rhythm, delivery, and available
      velocity well enough to begin and complete the load on time.
    </p>

    <p>
      Once the ball is released, the hitter begins gathering as much
      information as possible.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Speed</li>
      <li>Location</li>
      <li>Spin</li>
      <li>Changes in elevation</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      If the hitter is still trying to complete the load while identifying the
      pitch, valuable decision-making time has already been lost.
    </p>

    <p class="callout">
      The load prepares the body. The eyes prepare the decision.
    </p>

    <h3>In Game Situations</h3>

    <p>
      Preparation begins before the hitter steps into the batter's box.
    </p>

    <p>
      Watch the pitcher while he is warming up. Study his rhythm and begin
      rehearsing your own load.
    </p>

    <p>
      Continue practicing it while you are on deck.
    </p>

    <p>
      When you step into the box, your load should not be something you are
      trying to figure out.
    </p>

    <p>
      It should already feel familiar.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Drills</h2>

    <div class="drill-grid">
      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Load &amp; Hold</h3>
        <p>
          Lift into the load and briefly hover while reading the pitch. Then
          land and hold a balanced, neutral position that is ready to attack.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Rear Leg Pressure Drill</h3>
        <p>
          Place the back foot 8–12 inches up a wall and the front foot in a
          strided position. Push through the back foot while the front leg
          accepts and resists the force.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Step Back</h3>
        <p>
          Use a controlled step backward to feel the body gather before moving
          forward into the stride.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Walk Through</h3>
        <p>
          Use continuous movement to create rhythm and prevent the load from
          becoming stiff or stagnant.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Med Ball Catch</h3>
        <p>
          Catch a light medicine ball at different moments during the load.
          The hitter should remain athletic enough to stabilize without falling
          backward or lunging forward.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Push Test</h3>
        <p>
          A partner applies light pressure during the load to test whether the
          hitter remains centered, stable, and capable of continuing the
          movement.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="drill-card">
        <h3>Eyes Closed</h3>
        <p>
          Remove visual feedback and use body awareness to feel whether the
          load remains controlled and balanced.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Hands positioned too low.</li>
      <li>Hands positioned too high.</li>
      <li>Remaining stagnant before the swing.</li>
      <li>Drifting too far onto the back side.</li>
      <li>Lunging toward the pitcher.</li>
      <li>Landing too open to fire the hips efficiently.</li>
      <li>Landing and firing every body part at the same time.</li>
      <li>Over-coiling the body.</li>
      <li>Over-turning the head and eyes away from the pitcher.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="callout">
      Does the movement make the next pillar stronger or weaker?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Self Evaluation</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Is my load complete before I need to decide whether to swing?</li>
      <li>Can I remain athletic throughout the entire load?</li>
      <li>Is my weight controlled near my center of mass?</li>
      <li>Can I create force without sacrificing balance?</li>
      <li>Do my hands support an efficient barrel path?</li>
      <li>Am I prepared to fire efficiently from the ground up at heel plant?</li>
      <li>Does my load strengthen every pillar that follows?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</DeepDiveLayout>